president biden is calling jerome powell  into his office the principal's office  for a meeting today we have some context  that we can provide here via a quote  president joe biden will hold a rare  oval office meeting on tuesday with  federal reserve chair jerome powell amid  the highest inflation in decades which  has angered americans and hurt his  standing with voters the two will  discuss the state of the american and  global economy according to a white  house statement it's the first meeting  between the two since biden in november  announced his intention to nominate  powell for a second term at the helm of  the u.s central bank according to a  record of the fed chief's public  schedule which is available through  march so why is he doing this well it's  pretty obvious inflation is really bad  in this country if you look at what's  happening just from a wage standpoint  wages have gone up but they have not  outpaced inflation and inflation  actually gave the average worker a 2.4  pay cut last year and if you look at  this in the context of global inflation  rates the u.s ain't doing so hot  australia is at 5.1 percent norway 5.4  sweden 6.4 all the way denmark canada  eurozone the us is at 8.3 percent just  slightly below the uk  and then we have the price index here  year over year we have now just seen the  first tick down right there on the  bottom right since november 20. but if  you look at the actual price index we  are at the highest level since 1982  we've seen 16 consecutive increases in  the inflation rate  inflation again is really bad and if you  look at this for a bunch of different uh  things like gas prices look at gas  prices the national average for gas  prices per gallon is up towards four  dollars and fifty cents per gallon we  haven't seen these numbers in a long  long long long long long time actually  never  california's record high gas prices  today 6.62 cents from scott budman a  year ago that price was four dollars and  16 cents and in some places gas has now  gone over seven dollars closing at seven  dollars and thirty cents per gallon food  at home is just as bad and we've also  seen house appreciation home price  appreciation versus inflation massively  outpaced look at this 130 percent over  inflation that is numbers that we have  never even seen close to right we got  close to those numbers somewhat i guess  99  in the 2008  housing crisis and then if you look at  this we're up over 130 now compared to  inflation rates from a house  appreciation standpoint so the economy  has gone bananas people are out of  control food is more expensive gas is  more expensive housing is more expensive  all these things around you are more  expensive so even with wages up slightly  over the last few years  it's not outpacing inflation it's gotten  really bad and why is president biden  doing this you have to think about the  incentives right midterm elections are  coming up it's obviously not good for a  sitting u.s president to have the  highest inflation that we've seen in 40  plus years and if you think about this  in context of what could happen he's  calling him in to determine what will  happen next right they're trying to  figure out what exactly to do so i think  that there's a bunch of different things  to be discussed here i'm sure that this  will be a somewhat contentious meeting i  don't think that anyone is uh arguing  that they are uh doing the right thing  to some instance they're putting in an  extremely difficult position now with  covet and now getting themselves out of  that we have  high inflation and now most people  believe that we are going into a slowing  or shrinking economy in a recession so  jerome powell president biden they're  both in difficult positions they're  calling a meeting to discuss this this  doesn't happen very often but i think  that there will probably be  some blame cast or some decisions to be  made around what will happen next what  is your your take away from this john i  love that quote call him into the  principal's office that's essentially  what's happening here right inflation is  here they can't hide it anymore it's  across the board on almost everything  you buy food at home food away from home  shelter literally anything that you do  it's more expensive now than it has been  in the past i think that jerome powell  um is being called into biden's office  essentially saying hey what the hell is  going on what are we gonna do to fix  this um and then this is gonna be a  caveat like do they do what they say  they're gonna do and what they say  they're going to do does it work right  so do we get to that point where it's  hey  look we're going to raise rates okay  does raising rates work or are we going  to still tank the economy i think it's  kind of just an overview um but yeah you  went through all the inflation numbers  8.3 inflation we're  we're one of the worst in the world to  be honest at this point we're definitely  like bottom 50 bottom 10 in the world uh  inflation europe just had really high  inflation numbers come out as well but  everything across the board is expensive  gas which is i believe what most  americans are probably thinking about as  some are coming up trips are coming up  seven dollars a gallon some places 662  in california that's insane it was four  dollars last year yeah i i think that  what we need to avoid though is pricing  all the blame on specific individuals  obviously these are the figureheads in  most cases they're uh either elected or  not elected depending on who we're  talking about in this situation but  ultimately it's it's impossible to tell  what would have been different if  someone else is in there the last two  administrations have printed a bunch of  money that's just a fact right and  they've injected a bunch of money into  the global economy and what we see now  is this massive influx of inflation at  the end of the day though what i think  is important to remember is everyone  just needs to look out for themselves  like we've talked about on the show for  a long time many many many other people  outside of mainstream media we saw them  spin the narrative for a long time that  it was transit it was non-existent it  was transferred then it was good for you  right and there have been many people  here on this show elsewhere that have  talked about inflation for a long time  now for over 12 months and said that not  only is it coming but it's going to be  really bad levels that we haven't seen  in decades and that's literally exactly  what happened so people can look at  bitcoin today and say oh it's not  inflation hedge that's not true right  bitcoin is up nearly 400 percent since  the since the pandemic started and  ultimately what it did was it front ran  inflation it saw all this money printed  going on it saw asset prices starting to  increase it knew that this was going to  happen all the investors that were  buying bitcoin and that's exactly what  happened it was up you know 400 it was 3  500 4 000 under 4 000 when we started  the pandemic and now we're sitting here  at 30 000 so it performed well in my  expectations to that i think now we're  trading in this range right because it's  become a risk asset closely correlated  with equities we'll see what happens  over time but at the end of the day  bitcoin aside everything else aside like  everyone needs to just look out for  themselves because now you're in the  position where maybe you got a three  percent race maybe you got a five  percent raise which would have been  considered net positive in another year  is now you're under water and you're  you're making less money than last year  relative to the goods and services uh  that you purchase every single year so i  think ultimately again  take care of yourself take care of your  family make sure you're educated about  these things get invested make sure you  can participate in the price increase in  assets so if what happens a year ago or  two years ago happens again you're able  to see that increase in prices because  at the end of the day inflation is the  silent killer it's a tax on everyone and  it can put you in a really bad financial  position if you're not prepared to to  benefit from it from an asset  appreciation step

